# OpenCV without the GUI (highgui) backend — production image only.
#
# insightface hard-declares `opencv-python`, so the audited lock necessarily
# resolves the GUI build. That build links libGL.so.1, and on Debian trixie
# `libgl1` hard-depends on libglx-mesa0 -> mesa-libgallium -> libllvm19. The
# result was 192 MB of software OpenGL renderer (LLVM included) inside a
# headless server image, to support a highgui module nothing calls — there is
# no imshow/waitKey/namedWindow anywhere in the tree.
#
# The production image installs the audited set, uninstalls the GUI build, then
# reinstalls the same OpenCV version from this file. This file is pinned and
# hash-verified like the others, so B2 still holds: nothing reaches the image
# except from an audited lock.
#
# This is deliberately NOT merged into requirements-ai.in. Both distributions
# install the same `cv2/` directory, so having pip resolve them together makes
# the winner depend on install order.
#
# The version must match opencv-python in requirements.txt — bump both together.

-c requirements.txt

opencv-python-headless==5.0.0.93
